// Test simfile with hand-worked rhythm and tempo statistics, for calibrating ddr-trainer's
// chart analyzer (src/chart_analyzer.rs), whose tests check the tables below. Generated by
// test_resources/make_test_simfiles.py, so edit that instead of this.
//
// The BPM changes and stops, which all the charts share (each chart has its own stretch):
//   beat   0    20   24  26   28   29   32   36
//   BPM    120  240  60  120  180  120  480  120
//   stops  0.5 s on beat 38, 0.75 s on 40.5, 1.5 s on 42, 1 s on 44, 0.25 s on 48.5 and on
//          48.75, 2 s on 50
//
// Rhythm, from where the notes sit against the beat (Easy and Medium, at 120 BPM):
//   - an off-beat note is on any subdivision but 4
//   - a syncopation is an off-beat note with no note on the next stronger position after
//     it: the next beat after an 8th or a 12th, the next 8th after a 16th, the next 12th
//     after a 24th. So an 8th before a note on the beat isn't one, nor the 16th of a gallop,
//     nor a triplet that runs to the next beat. Its speed is 1 over the gap before it
//   - the rhythm changes on a note whose gaps before and after aren't even: the longer is
//     more than 1.1 times the shorter, in seconds. Its speed is 1 over the shorter
//   - the rhythm entropy is that of the gaps from each note to the next, in beats: with n
//     gaps, c of them of a length, the sum over the lengths of -(c/n) log2(c/n). 0 if the
//     gaps are all alike, 1 for two lengths as common as each other
// Count, then the speeds' mean, median and max:
//   chart      notes  off-beat  syncopations      rhythm changes    entropy
//   Beginner    0     0         0                 0                 0       nothing in it
//   Easy       23     11/23     6 (17/6, 8/3, 4)  10 (74/15, 4, 8)  2.2610
//   Medium     12     1/3       0                 1 (4, 4, 4)       0.8454
//   Hard       15     0         0                 5 (2.8, 2, 4)             see below
//
// Easy's notes by beat, with S under a syncopation and C under a rhythm change:
//   measure 0   0     1      2     3
//   measure 1   4     4.5    5     5.5    6.5     7.5
//               C                  S C    S       C
//   measure 2   8     8.75   9.5   10.25  11
//               C     S      S     S      C
//   measure 3   12    12.75  13    14     14 1/3  14 2/3  15  15 2/3
//               C     C      C     C                      C   S
//   Syncopations: 5.5 and 6.5 (nothing on 6 or 7, while 4.5 reaches 5 and 7.5 reaches 8),
//   the dotted 8ths 8.75, 9.5 and 10.25 (nothing on 9, 10 or 10.5), and 15 2/3 (nothing on
//   16). 12.75 reaches 13, and the triplet reaches 15. Their gaps before are 0.25, 0.5,
//   0.375 (three times) and 1/3 s: speeds 4, 2, 8/3, 8/3, 8/3 and 3.
//   Rhythm changes, each with the shorter of its gaps in beats: 4, 5.5, 7.5 and 8 (1/2), 11
//   and 12 (3/4), 12.75 and 13 (1/4), 14 and 15 (1/3). Speeds 4 (four times), 8/3 (twice),
//   8 (twice) and 6 (twice).
//   Its 22 gaps: 8 of a beat, 5 of 3/4, 4 of 1/2, 3 of 1/3, 1 of 1/4 and 1 of 2/3.
// Medium: 8ths from beat 0, then quarter notes from 4. Every 8th reaches a beat. The rhythm
//   changes on beat 4, between gaps of 1/2 and 1 beat. 8 gaps of 1/2 and 3 of 1.
//
// Compression: how many times smaller LZW makes the notes, as a stand-in for entropy that
// sees their order. Each note is two bytes: its gap (48ths of a beat since the note before,
// 255 at most, 0 for the first note) and its arrows (2 bits a panel from Left: 1 a tap, 2 a
// hold head, 3 a roll head). LZW starts with the 256 single bytes as its phrases. Each code
// is the longest phrase that comes next, in as many bits as it takes to number the phrases
// so far (8 for the first code, 9 from 257 phrases to 512), and adds that phrase with the
// byte after it. The fold is 8 bits a byte over the bits of the codes, for the steps (both
// bytes of each note), for the arrows alone and for the rhythm alone (the gaps).
//
//   chart     steps             arrows          rhythm
//   Beginner  0                 0               0               nothing in it
//   Medium    192/179 = 1.073   96/71 = 1.352   96/62 = 1.548
//
//   Medium's arrows are Left, Down, Up, Right three times (L D U R below), its gaps 0, then
//   24 eight times and 48 three times (0 e q below). The codes, | between them:
//   arrows  L | D | U | R | L D | U R | L D U | R                   8 codes: 8 + 7 x 9 bits
//   rhythm  0 | e | e e | e e e | e e | q | q q                     7 codes: 8 + 6 x 9 bits
//   steps   0 | L | e | D | e | U | e | R | e | L e | D e | U e | R e | L | q | D | q | U |
//           q | R                                                  20 codes: 8 + 19 x 9 bits
//   The steps come out barely smaller: a gap and its arrows are rarely a phrase it has seen.
//
// Tempo as the notes meet it (Hard: quarter notes on beats 16 to 27, 30, 31 and 32):
//   - each note is at the BPM in effect on its beat: 120 (16 to 19), 240 (20 to 23), 60 (24
//     and 25), 120 (26, 27, 30 and 31) and 480 (32). The 180 from 28 to 29 has no note in it
//   - a speedup is a note at a higher BPM than the note before, a slowdown one at a lower,
//     each measured in octaves: 1 for twice the BPM or half, 2 for four times or a quarter.
//     The travel is all of them added up
//
//   chart  mean  median  min  max  speedups  slowdowns  travel  max speedup  max slowdown
//   Hard   168   120     60   480  3         1          6       2            2
//
//   8 notes at 120, 4 at 240, 2 at 60 and 1 at 480: 2520/15. Speedups on beats 20 (1
//   octave), 26 (1) and 32 (2), a slowdown on 24 (2). The BPM changes 5 times between the
//   first note and the last (20, 24, 26, 28, 29), and the notes meet 4 changes.
//   Its rhythm changes though every note is a quarter note, since gaps are in seconds: on
//   beats 20 (0.5 s, then 0.25), 24 (0.25, 1), 26 (1, 0.5), 27 (0.5, 4/3) and 30 (4/3, 0.5),
//   at speeds 4, 4, 2, 2 and 2.
//   Easy and Medium are at 120 throughout: no speedups, no slowdowns.
//
// Stops in a run (Challenge: notes on beats 36 to 41, 44, 48, 49 and 50, at 120 BPM). A stop
// is in a run if the notes before and after it are 1 s apart at most, once the stops between
// them are taken out. A stop on a note's beat comes after the note.
//
//   chart      stops  seconds  in a run  seconds
//   Challenge  6      4.25     4         1.75
//
//   In a run: the 0.5 s on 38 (the notes on 38 and 39 are 0.5 s apart without it), the
//   0.75 s on 40.5 (40 and 41: 1.25 s apart with it, 0.5 s without) and both 0.25 s between
//   48 and 49. Not in one: the 1.5 s on 42 (41 and 44 are 1.5 s apart without it) and the
//   1 s on 44 (44 and 48 are 2 s apart). The 2 s on 50 is on the last note, which a stop
//   comes after, so it isn't counted at all. The other charts end before the first stop.
#TITLE:Rhythm Stats;
#ARTIST:ddr-trainer;
#MUSIC:rhythm_stats.ogg;
#OFFSET:0.000000;
#BPMS:0.000=120.000,20.000=240.000,24.000=60.000,26.000=120.000,28.000=180.000,
29.000=120.000,32.000=480.000,36.000=120.000;
#STOPS:38.000=0.500,40.500=0.750,42.000=1.500,44.000=1.000,48.500=0.250,48.750=0.250,
50.000=2.000;

